Third-party software used as dependencies (installed by pip, not included here)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
numpy (BSD-3-Clause and bundled licences), numba and llvmlite (BSD-2-Clause; llvmlite also
Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception), zstandard (BSD-3-Clause), pyppmd (LGPL-2.1-or-later, used
unmodified as a library). The comparison page and the benchmark (tools/compare_page/) also run
Node.js's built-in brotli and, optionally, paq8px.

paq8px (GPL-2.0-or-later) is NOT included. The benchmark can run it as a separate program that you
build yourself (https://github.com/hxim/paq8px) and point to with --paq8px.

Ideas, not code
---------------
The template model and the mixers use well-known context-mixing ideas from the PAQ family of
compressors by Matt Mahoney and contributors (lpaq1, paq8, paq8px) and the literature around them:
logistic mixing, adaptive bit counts, mixer weights selected by context, word classes. No code from
them was copied. The squash interpolation points (squash_knots in config/hebzip.toml) are computed
by hebzip/ops/knots.py from the logistic function.

Test data rebuilt by the user (not included)
---------------------------------------------
The benchmark's "real comments" set is rebuilt from the OMILab Hebrew sentiment benchmark
(https://github.com/omilab/Neural-Sentiment-Analyzer-for-Modern-Hebrew, MIT, (c) 2018 OMIlab, The Open
University of Israel; Amram, Ben-David, Tsarfaty, COLING 2018). Its text is not shipped.
